##
    The Fibonacci sequence, computed two ways.

    Both functions give the same answers, and the difference between them is the
    point. The recursive one mirrors the definition and is easier to read. The iterative one
    keeps two numbers and walks forward.

    The recursive version recomputes the same values over and over: asking for the thirtieth
    calls the twenty-eighth twice and the twenty-seventh three times. Raise the limit and watch
    where it stops being instant.
##

shared model Program

    constant integer Limit = 15;

    function Main()
        Console.WriteLine("n    recursive   iterative");

        loop for n = 0 to Program.Limit
            Console.WriteLine(
                Program.Pad(n) + Program.Pad(Program.Recursive(n)) + Program.Iterative(n));
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        @summary: The definition, written out.

        @remarks: Every recursion needs a case that does not recur, and here it is the first
        two numbers.

        @n: which number in the sequence to give back.
        @yields: the nth Fibonacci number.
    ##
    integer function Recursive(integer n)
        if n < 2
            yield n;
        end if

        yield Program.Recursive(n - 1) + Program.Recursive(n - 2);
    end function

    ##
        @summary: The same sequence, carrying only the two numbers it needs.
        @n: which number in the sequence to give back.
        @yields: the nth Fibonacci number.
    ##
    integer function Iterative(integer n)
        integer previous = 0;
        integer current = 1;

        loop for i = 0 until n
            integer next = previous + current;
            previous = current;
            current = next;
        end loop

        yield previous;
    end function

    ##
        @summary: A number followed by enough spaces to line the columns up.
        @value: the number to write.
        @yields: the number as text, padded to twelve characters.
    ##
    string function Pad(integer value)
        let text = "" + value;
        string padded = text;

        loop for i = text.Count() until 12
            padded = padded + " ";
        end loop

        yield padded;
    end function

end model
